We first recall that  any strongly convex function $h$ with
convexity constant $\sigma_h$ and having the optimal point $x^*$
satisfies the following inequality~\cite{Nes:04}: $h(x) \geq  h(x^*) + \frac{\sigma_h}{2}\|x - x^*\|^2 \quad \forall x$. Since for any convex function $h$ and fixed  point $x$, the Moreau smoothing  function  $h_\gamma(y;x) = h(y) +  \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \|y -x\|^2$ is
strongly convex in the first argument $y$ with strong convexity constant
$1/\gamma$, the following holds for all $x, y \in \rset^n$:
\begin{align}
\label{rel1_prox} 
& h(y)  \!+\!  \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \|y - x\|^2   \!\geq\!  h(\prox_{\gamma h}(x)) \!+\! \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \|\prox_{\gamma h}(x)
- x\|^2 \!+\! \frac{1}{2 \gamma} \|\prox_{\gamma h}(x) - y\|^2. 
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}
